From 30d6d10e554a720d55612e439e8513dae3d26fa1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 20:51:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Stop dismissing a role from destroying its match and
 application
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

job_matches and job_applications both declare `references job_postings(id) on
delete cascade`, and dismissing deleted the posting row. So throwing a role
away also threw away its match write-up, its application status and any
generated cover letter. Dismiss something you had already applied to and your
own pipeline entry went with it. The Supabase project is shared with five other
apps, so the day a second person is granted 'job' it gets worse: the posting is
one shared row, and one person dismissing a role would destroy everybody else's
verdicts and applications for it.

The delete was never what made dismissal work. The suppression row in
job_dismissed does the whole job on both paths that matter: the list filters
against it the moment it is written, so the card goes immediately, and the scan
filters re-imported feed results against it, so the role never comes back.
Neither ever consulted job_postings. Deleting the posting only tidied the table,
at the cost of cascading through other people's data.

Dismissed postings now stay in job_postings, inert — filtered out of every list
and never re-scored. That costs some rows and nothing else.

convergeLegacyFingerprints still deletes superseded duplicates, which is
correct: it already checks job_applications and keeps anything with one
attached.

schema.sql was a landmine, and is the reason this commit touches more than two
files. It is documented as safe to re-run, and re-running it would have undone
today's work on the live project: every policy recreated as `using (true)`,
which on a shared project means anyone with an account on any of the six apps;
job_ranked recreated without security_invoker, reopening a bypass that had all
1,337 postings and match write-ups readable by accounts with no grant; and the
DELETE policy restored, making this same cascade reachable from the browser
again. Its comment stated the belief that caused that hole — "views run with
the definer's rights; the underlying tables are still RLS-protected" — which is
backwards, and is why the view now carries security_invoker explicitly and says
why. There is deliberately no DELETE policy on job_postings now: nothing in the
browser deletes a posting, and with no policy nothing can.

The policies depend on has_app_access() from the platform repo, so the file
checks for it up front and refuses with an explanation rather than creating
policies that reference a missing function and lock everyone out.

Verified against a local Postgres with the platform layer in place: applies
clean, is idempotent across two runs, produces seven policies all gated on
has_app_access('job') and no DELETE policy, and leaves job_ranked with
security_invoker true. Against a database without the platform layer it fails
loudly and creates nothing. `npm run build` passes, which is the whole of this
repo's CI — there is no test suite here.

